<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Denis Diderot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1713">1713</a>–<a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Diderot</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Denis Diderot was a French philosopher and writer, a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. For over twenty years he edited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a>, overcoming bans and censorship to complete it. He also wrote novels, plays and art criticism, and his materialist and sceptical views made him a target of church and state. In 1773–1774 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, who had bought his library.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denis-diderot.jpg" alt="Denis Diderot" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denis_Diderot_by_Louis-Michel_van_Loo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
